    "seo": {
      "title": "Regional Sports of Iran: Local Identity and Wrestling Traditions",
      "description": "Discover selected regional sporting traditions in Iran, including Gilan's wrestling culture and Chouke's documented institutional development."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "regional-perspective",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading sport through place",
        "content": "Regional sport becomes clearer when the contest is considered alongside its audience, venue, and occasion. Christian Bromberger's account of Gilan, a province on Iran's Caspian coast, treats games as expressions of regional identity. Wrestling, tightrope performances, and other spectacles could bring audiences together on local commons or in household courtyards during celebrations. A sporting tradition consequently includes the social setting that makes a performance recognizable to its community. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "gilan-local-repertoire",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Gilan: wrestling within a wider performance culture",
        "content": "Bromberger identifies koshti gilamardi, or Gilani wrestling, within a local repertoire that also includes tightrope walking, egg contests, and arranged bull fights. These are distinct activities, not interchangeable wrestling disciplines. Their inclusion together in an ethnographic account reflects shared audiences and festive settings rather than identical rules or purposes. [s1]\n\nThe same account connects local games with weddings and other important visits or celebrations. It also reports changes associated with agricultural modernization and the spread of other sports. Those observations belong to the period documented by the author; they do not establish a measured rate of decline through the editorial cutoff. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "chouke-institutional-development",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Chouke and a new organizational framework",
        "content": "A more recent institutional perspective comes from United World Wrestling, the international wrestling federation. In April 2025 it announced a four-year memorandum with the Iran Wrestling Federation to promote and protect Chouke under its Associated Styles framework. The announcement records an agreement and its objectives; it does not by itself demonstrate increased participation or successful preservation. [s2]\n\nUWW describes competitors wrestling barefoot in a marked circular ring on mud, wearing a jacket and a cloth belt. Its account restricts grips to above the waist and prohibits touching the opponent's legs. These details identify the version presented by UWW. They should not be extended automatically to every local competition or historical practice bearing a related name. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "comparing-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What the comparison reveals",
        "content": "The sources answer different questions. Ethnography explains how sport participates in local social life; a federation announcement describes an organizational initiative and a competition format. Read together, they show why preserving regional sport requires attention both to community settings and to the rules used in formal competition. Neither source provides a complete national map of regional sports. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
    ],
